 742	// landsInGitAdministrativeDirectory reports the git administrative directory a resolved landing is
 743	// inside, whatever that directory is NAMED, and whether it found one.
 744	//
 745	// sem.PathLandsInGitDir above answers a question about a NAME: it refuses a landing that spells a
 746	// `.git` component. That is the only shape it can see, and git does not require that shape. A
 747	// repository created with `git init --separate-git-dir=admin` keeps a `.git` POINTER FILE holding
 748	// `gitdir: <path>` and its real administrative directory somewhere else; a repository initialised
 749	// under GIT_DIR has no `.git` entry at all. In both, `git rev-parse --absolute-git-dir` names a
 750	// directory whose components spell nothing the name rule recognises, so every route the name rule
 751	// closes reopens. Measured on this branch before this check, in a `--separate-git-dir` repository
 752	// whose administrative directory is `admin/`: a committed `CLAUDE.md -> admin/config` exited 0 with
 753	// the managed block appended to git's real config, after which every git command failed with
 754	// "fatal: bad config line 9"; `.entire -> admin/refs/heads` wrote the guide into the real ref store
 755	// and git reported "ignoring broken ref refs/heads/graph-agent.md"; and `AGENTS.md ->
 756	// admin/hooks/pre-commit` appended to an executable hook.
 757	//
 758	// The question is put to the FILESYSTEM rather than to `git rev-parse`, and re-asked at every
 759	// resolution rather than answered once at startup. Both follow from the rule the rest of this file
 760	// already holds itself to: the preflight is not the enforcement, so a directory that becomes a git
 761	// directory between two operations must be refused by the operation that finds it, which a value
 762	// cached at startup cannot do. Asking git would also make the answer depend on a binary being
 763	// installed, on $GIT_DIR in this process's environment, and on the current working directory —
 764	// none of which describe the repository named by --repo.
 765	//
 766	// What is asked is git's own is_git_directory(): a HEAD, plus objects/ and refs/. The
 767	// commondir/gitdir alternative admits a linked worktree's administrative directory, which keeps
 768	// those two pointer files in place of its own object and ref stores.
 769	//
 770	// The `.git` pointer file's TEXT is deliberately not consulted. sem's gitDirExcluder — the READ
 771	// side of this same boundary, which already resolves separate-git-dir pointers — records why: the
 772	// pointer is attacker-controlled input, so git resolves it and then asks is_git_directory() of the
 773	// target anyway, and a `gitdir:` naming an ordinary directory is "fatal: not a git repository" to
 774	// git rather than a git directory. Once that structure test is applied, the pointer says nothing
 775	// this walk has not already asked: a landing inside the target has the target among its own
 776	// ancestors, and a target outside the repository is refused by os.Root before it gets here. Reading
 777	// the text without the structure test would only add a way for a repository to make an ordinary
 778	// directory of its own unwritable.
 779	func landsInGitAdministrativeDirectory(root *os.Root, resolved string) (string, bool) {
 780		components := splitPathComponents(resolved)
 781		for i := 1; i <= len(components); i++ {
 782			prefix := filepath.Join(components[:i]...)
 783			if prefix == "" || prefix == "." {
 784				continue
 785			}
 786			if isGitAdministrativeDirectory(root, prefix) {
 787				return prefix, true
 788			}
 789		}
 790		return "", false
 791	}
